\chapter{Introduction}\label{ch:intro}

\section{Squarefree numbers in arithmetic progressions}

Let $\mu$ be the Möbius function, so that $\mu^2(n)$ is the indicator function of the squarefree positive integers. For a modulus $q\ge1$, an integer $l$ and real $x\ge1$ put
\[
Q(x;q,l)=\#\{n\le x:\ n\equiv l\ (\mathrm{mod}\ q),\ n\text{ squarefree}\}.
\]
The limit $g(q,l)=\lim_{x\to\infty}Q(x;q,l)/x$ exists; its closed form is recalled in \cref{prop:density}. Unlike the primes, the squarefree numbers populate residue classes that are \emph{not} coprime to the modulus, and their density $g(q,l)$ depends on $l$ through the common divisors of $l$ and $q$. For instance, $g(q,l)=0$ whenever $p^2$ divides both $q$ and $l$ for some prime $p$. The error term
\[
E(x;q,l)=Q(x;q,l)-g(q,l)\,x
\]
measures the irregularity of the distribution, and its mean square over all residue classes and all moduli up to $Y$,
\[
V(x,Y)=\sum_{q\le Y}\sum_{l=1}^{q}E(x;q,l)^2,
\]
is the squarefree analogue of the Barban--Davenport--Halberstam and Montgomery--Hooley variance for primes \cite{montgomery1970,hooley1975i}.

For $Y\le x$ the bound $V(x,Y)\ll_\eps x^{1+\eps}Y$ is Lemma~2.2 of Brüdern, Granville, Perelli, Vaughan and Wooley \cite{bgpvw1998} (with their parameter $k=2$); it is quoted precisely in \cref{quoted:bgpvw} below. Asymptotic formulae for $V(x,Y)$ go back to work of Orr, Warlimont and Croft, surveyed in the introduction of Vaughan's paper \cite{vaughan2005}; according to that survey, Warlimont \cite{warlimont1980} proved $V(x,Y)=x^2\mathcal F(x/Y)+O(x^{3/2}(\log x)^{7/2})$ for $1\le Y\le x$, with $\mathcal F(u)\sim c_2\,u^{-3/2}$ as $u\to\infty$, where $c_2$ is the constant in \eqref{eq:c2} below; and the argument of Croft \cite{croft1975} contains an error affecting the constant. Vaughan \cite[Theorem]{vaughan2005} broke the barrier $x^{3/2}$ inherent in the earlier methods. For squarefree numbers (his $k=2$) his theorem states that, for $Y\le x$,
\begin{multline}\label{eq:vaughan}
V(x,Y)=c_2x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}+O\Bigl(x^{1/4}Y^{7/4}\exp\Bigl(-c^*\frac{(\log 2x/Y)^{3/5}}{(\log\log 3x/Y)^{1/5}}\Bigr)\Bigr)\\
+O\Bigl(x^{3/2}\exp\Bigl(-\tilde c\frac{(\log x)^{3/5}}{(\log\log x)^{1/5}}\Bigr)\Bigr),
\end{multline}
for certain positive constants $c^*,\tilde c$, where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:c2}
c_2=\frac{8C_1\,(-\zeta(-\frac12))}{3\,\zeta(2)^2},\qquad
C_1=\prod_p\Bigl(1+\frac{(p-1)^2p^{3/2}-2p^4+3p^3-p}{p^2(p^2-1)^2}\Bigr)
\end{equation}
\cite[(1.13)--(1.15)]{vaughan2005}; in the numerator of the local factor of $C_1$, the term $3p^3$ is the sum $p^{2k-1}+2p^{k+1}$ of Vaughan's general formula at $k=2$. The main term is thus of order $x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$, much smaller than the ``diagonal'' size $xY$ one might naively expect; this reflects strong cancellation, which will also be the central feature of the present work.

\section{Sparse moduli}

Brüdern and Wooley \cite{bruedernwooley2011} asked the analogous question for primes when the moduli are restricted to the values $f(k)$ of a polynomial, weighting the modulus $f(k)$ by $f'(k)$. The weight makes the sparse sum behave like the full sum over moduli $q\le f(y)$, since $f'(k)\,\dd k=\dd f(k)$. They established an asymptotic formula of Montgomery--Hooley type for this sparse variance, improving the upper bound of Mikawa and Peneva \cite{mikawapeneva2005}. Their method follows the approach of Goldston and Vaughan \cite{goldstonvaughan1997} and Vaughan \cite{vaughan1998a}: the variance is linked to the number of solutions of $p_1-p_2=hf(k)$, which is then counted by the circle method.

This document treats the analogous problem for squarefree numbers. Throughout, $f\in\Z[t]$ is a polynomial of degree $d\ge1$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:f-hyp}
f(t)\ge2\quad\text{and}\quad f'(t)\ge1\qquad\text{for all real }t\ge1,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Vf}
V_f(x,y)=\sum_{k\le y}f'(k)\sum_{l=1}^{f(k)}E\bigl(x;f(k),l\bigr)^2\qquad(f(y)\le x).
\end{equation}
Here $k$ runs over positive integers, $y\ge1$ is real, and all residue classes modulo $f(k)$ are included. We write
\[
\rho(m)=\#\{a\bmod m:\ f(a)\equiv0\ (\mathrm{mod}\ m)\},\qquad \rho(1)=1,
\]
and $Y=f(y)$. No irreducibility, squarefreeness or discriminant condition is imposed on $f$, and $f$ may have fixed prime divisors.

\section{The main result}

To state the result we need an arithmetic weight. For a positive integer $h$ and a prime $p$ let $v_p(h)$ be the exponent of $p$ in $h$, and define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:W-intro}
W_f(h)=\prod_p w_p(h),\qquad
w_p(h)=\begin{cases}
1-\dfrac2{p^2}+\dfrac{\rho(p^2)}{p^4},& p\nmid h,\\[2mm]
1-\dfrac2{p^2}+\dfrac{\rho(p)}{p^3},& p\parallel h,\\[2mm]
1-\dfrac1{p^2},& p^2\mid h.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Equivalently, $w_p(h)=(1-2p^{-2})\bigl(1+\rho(p^{j})/(p^{j}(p^2-2))\bigr)$ with $j=\max\{2-v_p(h),0\}$. In \cref{ch:averaging} we shall see that $W_f(h)$ is the average over $k$ of the singular series $\Sing(hf(k))$ that governs the number of pairs of squarefree integers with difference $hf(k)$. The product converges absolutely and $0<W_f(h)\le1$ (\cref{lem:W-basic}). Let
\[
D_f(s)=\sum_{h\ge1}W_f(h)h^{-s}\qquad(\re s>1).
\]
By \cref{prop:Df}, $D_f$ continues meromorphically to $\re s>-\frac34$, where its only poles are simple poles at $s=1$ and $s=-\frac12$. Define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:RBK}
R_f=\Res_{s=1}D_f(s),\qquad
B_f=\lim_{s\to1}\Bigl(D_f(s)-\frac{R_f}{s-1}\Bigr)-\frac32R_f,\qquad
K_f=-\frac{16}{3}\Res_{s=-1/2}D_f(s),
\end{equation}
and, for real $H\ge1$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ThetaF}
\Theta_f(H)=\sum_{1\le h\le H}W_f(h)\frac{(H-h)^2}{h},\qquad
F_f(H)=R_f\log H+B_f+\frac{1}{\zeta(2)H}-\frac{\Theta_f(H)}{H^2}.
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]\label{thm:main}
Let $f$ satisfy \eqref{eq:f-hyp}, let $d=\deg f$, and put
\[
\eta_d=\min\Bigl\{\frac15,\frac1{4d-1}\Bigr\}.
\]
For every $\eps$ with $0<\eps<\eta_d/2$ there are constants $C(f,\eps)>0$ and $x_0(f,\eps)$ such that, uniformly for real $x\ge x_0(f,\eps)$ and real $y\ge1$ with $Y=f(y)\le x$,
\begin{align}
\bigl|V_f(x,y)-x^2F_f(x/Y)\bigr|&\le C(f,\eps)\,x^{2-\eta_d+\eps},\label{eq:main1}\\
\bigl|V_f(x,y)-K_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}\bigr|&\le C(f,\eps)\bigl(x^{3/8}Y^{13/8}+x^{2-\eta_d+\eps}\bigr).\label{eq:main2}
\end{align}
The constant $K_f$ is positive and is given by the absolutely convergent Euler product
\begin{equation}\label{eq:K-intro}
K_f=-\frac83\,\zeta(-\tfrac12)\prod_p\Bigl(1-\frac1p\Bigr)\Bigl(1+\frac1p-\frac{2+\rho(p)}{p^2}+\frac{\rho(p)}{p^{5/2}}-\frac{\rho(p^2)}{p^{7/2}}+\frac{\rho(p^2)}{p^4}\Bigr).
\end{equation}
Moreover
\begin{equation}\label{eq:R-intro}
R_f=\frac1{\zeta(2)^2}\prod_p\Bigl(1+\frac{(1-1/p)(\rho(p)+\rho(p^2))}{(p^2-1)^2}\Bigr).
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

The function $F_f$ is explicit, and \cref{prop:Theta} shows that $|F_f(H)-K_fH^{-3/2}|\le C(f)H^{-13/8}$ for all $H\ge1$; \eqref{eq:main2} is \eqref{eq:main1} combined with this expansion at $H=x/Y$. Dividing \eqref{eq:main2} by its main term gives the following.

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:range}
Let $0<\delta<\eta_d/3$. Uniformly for $x\ge x_0(f,\delta)$ and $x^{1-2\eta_d/3+\delta}\le f(y)\le x^{1-\delta}$,
\[
V_f(x,y)=K_f\,x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2}\bigl(1+O_{f,\delta}(x^{-\delta/8})\bigr).
\]
\end{corollary}

The proof of \cref{cor:range} is given in \cref{sec:corollary}. The exponents for small degrees are as follows.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}cccc@{}}
\toprule
$d$ & $\eta_d$ & error in \eqref{eq:main1} & range of \cref{cor:range}\\
\midrule
$1$ & $1/5$ & $x^{9/5+\eps}$ & $x^{13/15+\delta}\le f(y)\le x^{1-\delta}$\\
$2$ & $1/7$ & $x^{13/7+\eps}$ & $x^{19/21+\delta}\le f(y)\le x^{1-\delta}$\\
$3$ & $1/11$ & $x^{21/11+\eps}$ & $x^{31/33+\delta}\le f(y)\le x^{1-\delta}$\\
$d\ge2$ & $1/(4d-1)$ & $x^{2-1/(4d-1)+\eps}$ & $x^{1-2/(3(4d-1))+\delta}\le f(y)\le x^{1-\delta}$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{remark}[Two regimes]\label{rem:regimes}
\Cref{thm:main} distinguishes two descriptions of the variance. Suppose that $Y/x$ tends to zero and that, in addition, the additive error $x^{2-\eta_d+\eps}$ in \eqref{eq:main2} is $o(x^{1/2}Y^{3/2})$; this holds, for instance, throughout the range of \cref{cor:range}. Then \eqref{eq:main2} shows that the variance is asymptotic to the power law $K_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$, as in Vaughan's theorem \eqref{eq:vaughan}. For smaller $Y$ the theorem gives only an upper bound for the difference, and no asymptotic formula. When the ratio $r=Y/x$ is fixed, \eqref{eq:main1} gives $V_f(x,y)=x^2F_f(1/r)+O(x^{2-\eta_d+\eps})$. Here $x^2F_f(1/r)=K_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}\cdot F_f(1/r)r^{-3/2}/K_f$, and by \eqref{eq:F-asym} the factor $F_f(1/r)r^{-3/2}/K_f=1+O(r^{1/8})$ is independent of $x$. The theorem does not say that this factor equals one, and the numerical values in \cref{ch:numerics} show that it does not. The normalised quantity $V_f(x,y)/(x^{1/2}Y^{3/2})$ therefore approaches the ratio-dependent profile $F_f(1/r)r^{-3/2}$ rather than the constant $K_f$. This distinction is essential for interpreting the numerical data.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Consistency with Vaughan's constant]\label{rem:vaughan}
For $f(t)=t+1$ one has $\rho(m)=1$ for all $m$, and $V_f(x,y)=V(x,Y)-E(x;1,1)^2$ with $Y=f(y)=y+1$, because the moduli $f(k)=k+1$, $k\le y$, are exactly the integers $2\le q\le Y$; by \cref{prop:density} the subtracted term is $O(x)$. A direct computation shows that for $\rho(p)=\rho(p^2)=1$ each local factor in \eqref{eq:K-intro} equals $(1-p^{-2})^2$ times the local factor of $C_1$ in \eqref{eq:c2}. Since $\prod_p(1-p^{-2})^2=\zeta(2)^{-2}$, it follows that $K_{t+1}=c_2$. Thus the constant of \cref{thm:main} agrees with Vaughan's constant in the one case where both results apply; numerically $K_{t+1}=\KVau$ (see \cref{tab:constants}). For $f(t)=t+1$, \cref{thm:main} is of course much weaker than \eqref{eq:vaughan} as far as the range and the error term are concerned. This comparison was made while preparing this document and is not part of the archived proof (see \cref{app:provenance}).
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Limitations]
The asymptotic formula is established only for $f(y)\ge x^{1-2\eta_d/3+\delta}$, not throughout $f(y)\le x$. Two features of the argument fix the exponent $\eta_d$ (see \cref{sec:budget}). The cap $1/5$ comes from summing the error $O(x^{4/5})$ of the correlation estimate (\cref{prop:corr}) over about $x\log x$ pairs $(h,k)$. The exponent $1/(4d-1)$ comes from balancing the bound for small moduli, which is obtained by inserting the full variance bound of \cite{bgpvw1998}, against the error of the averaging over polynomial values in \cref{prop:averaging}.
\end{remark}

\section{Numerical illustration}

\Cref{ch:numerics} compares the theorem with an archived numerical snapshot of exact computations of $V_f(x,y)$ for $f(t)=t^2+1$, $t^3+2$ and $2t+1$, for $2.5\cdot10^5\le x\le 2.56\cdot10^8$ and $f(y)/x\in[0.005,1]$; the computations are repeated by the script \texttt{reproduce.py} accompanying this document. The constants are
\[
K_{t^2+1}=\KTwo,\qquad K_{t^3+2}=\KThree,\qquad K_{2t+1}=\KLin,
\]
computed from \eqref{eq:K-intro} with primes up to $\Pmax$. At the largest $x$ the data agree with the profile $x^2F_f(x/Y)$ to within a fraction of one per cent for $t^2+1$ and $2t+1$, and to within a few per cent for $t^3+2$, where $y$ is only a few hundred. The snapshot's empirical extrapolation to $r\to0$ is consistent with $K_f$ within its heuristic uncertainty envelope. \Cref{sec:extrapolation} splits its offset from $K_f$ into a part due to the fitting model itself and a part due to the difference between data and profile at finite $x$. The numbers are evidence, not proof; \cref{ch:numerics} explains the method and its limitations.

\section{Strategy of the proof}\label{sec:strategy}

The proof follows a classical route: expand the square, count pairs of squarefree numbers in the same residue class, and evaluate the resulting averages. It has seven steps, corresponding to \cref{ch:densities,ch:variance,ch:correlations,ch:averaging,ch:dirichlet,ch:mellin,ch:assembly}.

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item \emph{Local densities} (\cref{ch:densities}). We compute $g(q,l)$, the second moment $q\sum_lg(q,l)^2=\sum_{r\mid q}\lambda(r)$, and the Ramanujan expansion $\Sing(n)=\sum_r\gamma(r)^2c_r(n)$ of the singular series
\[
\Sing(n)=\prod_p\Bigl(1-\frac2{p^2}\Bigr)\prod_{p^2\mid n}\frac{p^2-1}{p^2-2}.
\]
\item \emph{Expanding the variance} (\cref{ch:variance}). Opening the square and counting ordered pairs of squarefree numbers in a common residue class gives
\[
V_f(x;z,y)\approx cx(Y-Z)+2\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)\sum_{h\le x/f(k)}C_x(hf(k))-x^2\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)\sum_{l}g(f(k),l)^2,
\]
where $c=\zeta(2)^{-1}$, $Z=f(z)$ and $C_x(m)=\sum_{n\le x-m}\mu^2(n)\mu^2(n+m)$. The moduli $f(k)$ with $k\le z$ are disposed of by the bound of \cite{bgpvw1998}.
\item \emph{Correlations} (\cref{ch:correlations}). An elementary argument gives $C_x(m)=(x-m)\Sing(m)+O(x^{4/5})$ uniformly in $1\le m\le x$.
\item \emph{Averaging over polynomial moduli} (\cref{ch:averaging}). Using the Ramanujan expansion, the dependence of $\Sing(hf(k))$ and of $\sum_lg(f(k),l)^2$ on $k$ is periodic modulo each $r$. Partial summation replaces the sums over $k$ by integrals over $t=f(k)$. The singular series average to $W_f(h)$, and the density term becomes $R_f\log(Y/Z)$, with the same constant $R_f$.
\item \emph{The Dirichlet series} (\cref{ch:dirichlet}). The factorisation $D_f(s)=\zeta(s)\zeta(2s+2)H_f(s)$ with $H_f$ an Euler product holomorphic for $\re s>-\frac34$ gives the residues and the value $D_f(0)=-\frac12\zeta(2)^{-1}$.
\item \emph{The smoothed sum} (\cref{ch:mellin}). A Mellin integral with kernel $2/(s(s+1)(s+2))$ and a contour shift to $\re s=-\frac58$ give
\[
\Theta_f(H)=R_fH^2\log H+B_fH^2+cH-K_fH^{1/2}+O(H^{3/8}).
\]
The term $cH$ comes from the pole of the kernel at $s=0$ and the value $D_f(0)$.
\item \emph{Assembly} (\cref{ch:assembly}). The integral produced in step (4) equals $\frac12\bigl(Z^2\Theta_f(x/Z)-Y^2\Theta_f(x/Y)\bigr)$ exactly. In the combination of steps (2)--(6), the terms $x^2R_f\log(Y/Z)$, the $B_f$-terms and the diagonal term $cx(Y-Z)$ cancel identically, which leaves $x^2F_f(x/Y)-x^2F_f(x/Z)$. Choosing $Z=x^{2d/(4d-1)}$ and estimating every error term proves \cref{thm:main}.
\end{enumerate}

The cancellation in step (7) is the reason for the shape of the profile $F_f$. All three large terms, of sizes $x^2\log x$, $x^2$ and $xY$, disappear, and what remains is $x^2F_f(x/Y)\approx K_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$, produced by the pole of $\zeta(2s+2)$ at $s=-\frac12$.

\section{Organisation and conventions of this document}

\Cref{ch:prelim} collects elementary facts used repeatedly: tail estimates, the divisor bound, infinite products, Ramanujan sums and the growth of $f$. Each of \cref{ch:densities,ch:variance,ch:correlations,ch:averaging,ch:dirichlet,ch:mellin,ch:assembly} proves one of the seven propositions of the archived proof; their statements are kept with the hypotheses, ranges and uniformity of the archive. \Cref{ch:numerics} describes the numerical evidence, \cref{app:tables} lists the full numerical tables, and \cref{app:provenance} records how this document was produced. The file \texttt{changes.md} maps every statement to its archived source.

Only two results are quoted from the literature: Lemma~2.2 of \cite{bgpvw1998}, used for the small moduli in \cref{prop:initial}, and Corollary~10.5 of \cite{montgomeryvaughan2007} (a consequence of the functional equation of $\zeta$), used to bound $\zeta$ in a vertical strip in \cref{lem:zeta-growth}. Everything else is proved here.

\chapter{Expanding the variance}\label{ch:variance}

We open the square in the definition of $V_f$ and separate three kinds of terms: a diagonal term, a mixed term involving the densities, and an off-diagonal term that counts pairs of squarefree numbers whose difference is a multiple of the modulus. The moduli $f(k)$ with $k$ below a cutoff $z$ are removed by a known upper bound. (Archived source: proposition P2.)

\section{Notation}

For real $x\ge2$ and integers $1\le m\le x$ put
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Cx}
C_x(m)=\sum_{1\le n\le x-m}\mu^2(n)\mu^2(n+m).
\end{equation}
For a modulus $q$ write $H(q)=\sum_{l=1}^qg(q,l)^2$. For real $1\le z\le y$ with $Z=f(z)\le Y=f(y)\le x$ define
\begin{align}
V_f(x;z,y)&=\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)\sum_{l=1}^{f(k)}E(x;f(k),l)^2,\label{eq:Vzy}\\
T_f(z,y)&=\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)\sum_{l=1}^{f(k)}g(f(k),l)^2=\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)H(f(k)),\label{eq:Tf}\\
S_f(x;z,y)&=\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)\sum_{1\le h\le x/f(k)}C_x(hf(k)).\label{eq:Sf}
\end{align}
Since $f$ is increasing, $f(k)\le Y\le x$ for $k\le y$, and every argument $hf(k)$ of $C_x$ in \eqref{eq:Sf} lies in $[1,x]$. Note that $V_f(x,y)=V_f(x,z)+V_f(x;z,y)$ for $1\le z\le y$.

\section{Counting pairs in a residue class}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:pairs}
For real $x\ge2$ and every integer $1\le q\le x$,
\[
\sum_{l=1}^qQ(x;q,l)^2=\sum_{n\le x}\mu^2(n)+2\sum_{1\le h\le x/q}C_x(hq).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The left side counts ordered pairs $(n_1,n_2)$ of squarefree integers in $[1,x]$ with $n_1\equiv n_2\bmod q$. Pairs with $n_1=n_2$ give the first term. For the others, the positive difference is $hq$ for exactly one integer $1\le h\le x/q$; the pairs with $n_2-n_1=hq$ are counted by $C_x(hq)$, and reversing the order gives the factor two.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:mixed}
For every integer $q\ge1$ and real $x\ge1$, the mixed sum
\[
\Delta(x;q)=\sum_{l=1}^qg(q,l)E(x;q,l)
\]
satisfies $|\Delta(x;q)|\le3\sqrt x\,\tau(q)/q$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Put $q_1=\prod_{p\parallel q}p$ and $A_q=\prod_{p\mid q}(1-p^{-2})^{-1}$. By \eqref{eq:c-euler}, $cA_q=\prod_{p\nmid q}(1-p^{-2})\le1$. For squarefree $n$ the zero case of \cref{prop:density} cannot occur, and expanding $\prod_{p\mid(q_1,n)}(1-p^{-1})$ in \eqref{eq:density-closed} gives
\[
\mu^2(n)g(q,n)=\frac{cA_q}q\mu^2(n)\sum_{a\mid q_1,\ a\mid n}\frac{\mu(a)}a.
\]
The squarefree $n\le x$ divisible by $a$ are counted by $Q(x;a,0)$, and \cref{prop:density} gives $|Q(x;a,0)-x\,g(a,0)|\le3\sqrt x$. Hence, with $B(q)=\frac{cA_q}q\sum_{a\mid q_1}\frac{\mu(a)}a\,g(a,0)$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:mixed-B}
\Bigl|\sum_{n\le x}\mu^2(n)g(q,n)-xB(q)\Bigr|\le\frac{3\sqrt x}q\sum_{a\mid q_1}\frac1a\le\frac{3\sqrt x\,\tau(q)}q.
\end{equation}
Since $g(q,n)$ depends only on $n\bmod q$,
$\sum_{n\le t}\mu^2(n)g(q,n)=\sum_{l=1}^qg(q,l)Q(t;q,l)$. Dividing by $t$ and letting $t\to\infty$, the right side tends to $H(q)$ and, by \eqref{eq:mixed-B}, the left side tends to $B(q)$. Thus $B(q)=H(q)$, and since $\Delta(x;q)=\sum_{l}g(q,l)Q(x;q,l)-xH(q)=\sum_{n\le x}\mu^2(n)g(q,n)-xH(q)$, the claim follows from \eqref{eq:mixed-B}.
\end{proof}

\section{The variance on an interval of moduli}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:reduction}
For every $\eps>0$ there is $C(f,\eps)>0$ such that, uniformly for real $x\ge2$ and $1\le z\le y$ with $Z=f(z)\le Y=f(y)\le x$,
\[
\Bigl|V_f(x;z,y)-\bigl[cx(Y-Z)+2S_f(x;z,y)-x^2T_f(z,y)\bigr]\Bigr|\le C(f,\eps)\bigl(x^{3/2+\eps}+xY^{1-1/d}\bigr).
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix $q=f(k)\le x$. Since $Q=E+xg$, expanding $\sum_lQ^2$ and using \cref{lem:pairs} and the definition of $\Delta$ gives the exact identity
\[
\sum_{l=1}^qE(x;q,l)^2=\sum_{n\le x}\mu^2(n)+2\sum_{1\le h\le x/q}C_x(hq)-x^2H(q)-2x\Delta(x;q).
\]
Multiply by $f'(k)$ and sum over $z<k\le y$. With $\mathcal W=\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)$, the difference to be estimated is exactly
\[
\mathcal R=\Bigl(\sum_{n\le x}\mu^2(n)-cx\Bigr)\mathcal W+cx\bigl(\mathcal W-(Y-Z)\bigr)-2x\sum_{z<k\le y}f'(k)\Delta(x;f(k)).
\]
\emph{Diagonal error.} \Cref{prop:density} with $q=1$ gives $|\sum_{n\le x}\mu^2(n)-cx|\le3\sqrt x$, since $g(1,0)=\sum_m\mu(m)m^{-2}=c$. By \eqref{eq:weight-count} and $Y\le x$, the first term is at most $3(1+K_1)\sqrt xY\le3(1+K_1)x^{3/2}$.

\emph{Endpoint error.} By \eqref{eq:weight-count}, the second term is at most $cK_1xY^{1-1/d}$.

\emph{Mixed error.} Let $\eta=\eps/2$. By \cref{lem:mixed}, \cref{lem:divisor} and $f(k)\le x$, each term satisfies $|f'(k)\Delta(x;f(k))|\le3D_\eta x^{1/2+\eta}f'(k)/f(k)$. Summing with \eqref{eq:sum-w} and using $\log2x\le J_\eta x^\eta$ (\cref{lem:sums}(d)), we find that the third term is at most
\[
2x\cdot3D_\eta x^{1/2+\eta}\cdot2U\log(2x)\le12D_\eta UJ_\eta\,x^{3/2+\eps}.
\]
Adding the three bounds proves the proposition with $C(f,\eps)=3(1+K_1)+12D_{\eps/2}UJ_{\eps/2}+cK_1$. The argument also covers $z=y$ and intervals containing no integer.
\end{proof}

\section{The initial moduli}

For the moduli $f(k)$ with $k\le z$ we use the full-variance bound of Brüdern, Granville, Perelli, Vaughan and Wooley. In their notation $\mu_k$ is the indicator of the $k$-free numbers, so $\mu_2=\mu^2$, and their parameter $k$ is unrelated to our summation variable.

\begin{quoted}[{\cite[Lemma~2.2 and (2.6)--(2.7)]{bgpvw1998}}]\label{quoted:bgpvw}
For positive integers $a,q$ define $E_k(x;q,a)$ by
\[
\sum_{\substack{1\le n\le x\\n\equiv a\ (\mathrm{mod}\ q)}}\mu_k(n)=\frac x{\zeta(k)}g_k(q,(q,a))+E_k(x;q,a),
\qquad
\Upsilon_k(x,Q)=\sum_{1\le q\le Q}\sum_{a=1}^q|E_k(x;q,a)|^2.
\]
Suppose that $Q$ and $x$ are positive real numbers and that $k\ge2$ is an integer. Then for each $\eps>0$,
\[
\Upsilon_k(x,Q)\ll x^{2/k+\eps}Q^{2-2/k}\quad(1\le Q\le x),\qquad\Upsilon_k(x,Q)\ll Q^2\log(2Q)\quad(Q>x).
\]
\end{quoted}

The inner sum in $\Upsilon_k$ runs over all residue classes, coprime to $q$ or not. We check that the normalisation agrees with ours without using the formula for $g_k$. Fix $1\le a\le q$ and apply the lemma with $k=2$, $Q=q$, $\eps=\frac12$ and $x=t\ge\max\{2,q\}$. By positivity, $|E_2(t;q,a)|^2\le\Upsilon_2(t,q)\ll t^{3/2}q$, so $E_2(t;q,a)/t\to0$ as $t\to\infty$. Dividing the definition by $t$ therefore shows that $\zeta(2)^{-1}g_2(q,(q,a))=\lim_tQ(t;q,a)/t=g(q,a)$. Hence $E_2=E$, and with $k=2$ the lemma states that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:full-variance}
V(x,Q)=\sum_{q\le Q}\sum_{l=1}^qE(x;q,l)^2\le B_\eps x^{1+\eps}Q\qquad(1\le Q\le x)
\end{equation}
for every $\eps>0$, where $B_\eps$ depends only on $\eps$. The same bound is recalled as (1.5) in \cite{vaughan2005}.

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:initial}
For every $\eps>0$ there is $C(f,\eps)$ such that, uniformly for real $x\ge2$ and real $z\ge1$ with $f(z)\le x$,
\[
0\le V_f(x,z)\le C(f,\eps)\,x^{1+\eps}f(z)^{2-1/d}.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $Z=f(z)$. By \cref{lem:poly}(a) the moduli $f(k)$, $k\le z$, are distinct integers in $[2,Z]$, and by \cref{lem:poly}(e) their weights satisfy $0<f'(k)\le\kappa_2\kappa_1^{-\alpha}Z^\alpha$ with $\alpha=1-1/d$. Each inner sum $\sum_lE^2$ is nonnegative. We may therefore first bound each weight by this maximum and then enlarge the set of moduli to all $q\le Z$:
\[
0\le V_f(x,z)\le\kappa_2\kappa_1^{-\alpha}Z^{\alpha}\sum_{q\le Z}\sum_{l=1}^qE(x;q,l)^2\le\kappa_2\kappa_1^{-\alpha}B_\eps\,x^{1+\eps}Z^{1+\alpha}.
\]
Here \eqref{eq:full-variance} is applicable because $1\le2\le Z\le x$, and $1+\alpha=2-1/d$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
It is essential to use positivity before replacing the weights by their maximum. Bounding a sparse sum by the full sum over all $q\le Z$ costs the factor $Z^{1-1/d}$. This loss is one of the two sources of the exponent $1/(4d-1)$ in \cref{thm:main}.
\end{remark}
\chapter{Correlations of squarefree numbers}\label{ch:correlations}

The off-diagonal term $S_f$ is a weighted sum of the correlations $C_x(m)$ of \eqref{eq:Cx}. We need an asymptotic formula for $C_x(m)$ that is uniform in the whole range $1\le m\le x$, including differences $m$ close to $x$, where the number $x-m$ of available pairs is small. Counts of pairs of squarefree numbers with a given difference have a long history (see for example \cite{mirsky1949}, and the discussion of the related binary additive problem in \cite[\S1]{bgpvw1998}). The archived proof gives a self-contained elementary argument with an explicit absolute constant. The archived plan anticipated a circle-method argument based on \cite{bgpvw1998}, but the proof does not need it. (Archived source: proposition P3.)

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:corr}
There is an absolute constant $K_{\mathrm c}$ such that for all real $x\ge2$ and all integers $1\le m\le x$,
\[
\bigl|C_x(m)-(x-m)\Sing(m)\bigr|\le K_{\mathrm c}\,x^{4/5}.
\]
One may take $K_{\mathrm c}=67+2D_{1/10}$, with $D_{1/10}$ as in \cref{lem:divisor}. In particular, for every $\eps>0$ the bound $C(\eps)x^{4/5+\eps}$ holds, which is the form recorded in the archived plan.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Put $L=x-m\ge0$ and $H=\lfloor x^{2/5}\rfloor$, so that $\frac12x^{2/5}\le H\le x^{2/5}$ and $H\ge1$. All bounds below hold for every real $L\ge0$, including $L<1$, when $C_x(m)=0$.

\emph{Truncation.} For $k\ge1$ split \eqref{eq:mu2} as $\mu^2(k)=T_H(k)+R_H(k)$, where
\[
T_H(k)=\sum_{\substack{e\le H\\e^2\mid k}}\mu(e),\qquad R_H(k)=\sum_{\substack{e>H\\e^2\mid k}}\mu(e).
\]
Then $|T_H(k)|\le\tau(k)$ and $|R_H(k)|\le\#\{e>H:e^2\mid k\}$. Each of the sets $\{n:1\le n\le L\}$ and $\{n+m:1\le n\le L\}$ consists of integers in $[1,x]$, because $m+L=x$. Each contains at most $x/e^2$ multiples of $e^2$. Therefore, by \cref{lem:sums}(a),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:RH-sum}
\sum_{1\le n\le L}|R_H(n)|\le\sum_{e>H}\frac x{e^2}\le\frac xH,\qquad\sum_{1\le n\le L}|R_H(n+m)|\le\frac xH.
\end{equation}
The exact identity
\[
\mu^2(n)\mu^2(n+m)-T_H(n)T_H(n+m)=R_H(n)\mu^2(n+m)+T_H(n)R_H(n+m)
\]
together with $0\le\mu^2\le1$ and $|T_H(n)|\le\tau(n)\le D_{1/10}x^{1/10}$ (\cref{lem:divisor}) bounds each summand by $|R_H(n)|+D_{1/10}x^{1/10}|R_H(n+m)|$. Summing with \eqref{eq:RH-sum},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:corr-trunc}
\Bigl|C_x(m)-\sum_{1\le n\le L}T_H(n)T_H(n+m)\Bigr|\le(1+D_{1/10}x^{1/10})\frac xH\le2x^{3/5}+2D_{1/10}x^{7/10}.
\end{equation}

\emph{Counting the truncated congruences.} Let $e_1,e_2\le H$ and $g=(e_1,e_2)$. The congruences $n\equiv0\bmod e_1^2$ and $n\equiv-m\bmod e_2^2$ are soluble exactly when $g^2\mid m$. Necessity follows by reducing modulo $g^2$. For sufficiency write $e_1=ga$, $e_2=gb$ with $(a,b)=1$ and $n=e_1^2u$; the second congruence becomes $a^2u\equiv-m/g^2\bmod b^2$, which determines $u$ modulo $b^2$. Hence the solutions form one residue class modulo $e_1^2e_2^2/g^2$. For any residue class modulo $h$ with representative $r$, the number of its members in $[1,L]$ is $\lfloor(L-r)/h\rfloor-\lfloor-r/h\rfloor=L/h+\theta$ with $|\theta|\le1$. Expanding both truncated sums therefore gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:corr-AH}
\sum_{1\le n\le L}T_H(n)T_H(n+m)=L\,\mathcal A_H(m)+E_H,\qquad|E_H|\le H^2\le x^{4/5},
\end{equation}
where
\[
\mathcal A_H(m)=\sum_{\substack{e_1,e_2\le H\\(e_1,e_2)^2\mid m}}\frac{\mu(e_1)\mu(e_2)(e_1,e_2)^2}{e_1^2e_2^2}.
\]

\emph{Completing the series.} Let $\mathcal A(m)$ denote the same sum over all $e_1,e_2\ge1$. Writing $e_1=ga$, $e_2=gb$ and dropping the coprimality of $a,b$ shows absolute convergence:
$\sum_{e_1,e_2}(e_1,e_2)^2/(e_1e_2)^2\le\sum_gg^{-2}\sum_aa^{-2}\sum_bb^{-2}\le8$. For the tail with $e_1>H$, the same substitution gives the bound $\sum_gg^{-2}\bigl(\sum_{a>H/g}a^{-2}\bigr)\bigl(\sum_bb^{-2}\bigr)$. For $g\le H$ the summand is at most $g^{-2}(2g/H)\cdot2=4/(gH)$ by \cref{lem:sums}(a), and summing over $g\le H$ gives at most $4(1+\log H)/H$. For $g>H$ the summand is at most $4/g^2$, and these sum to at most $4/H$. By symmetry the tail with $e_2>H$ has the same bound, so
\begin{equation}\label{eq:corr-tail}
|\mathcal A(m)-\mathcal A_H(m)|\le\frac{8(2+\log H)}H,
\end{equation}
uniformly in $m$, because the condition $(e_1,e_2)^2\mid m$ was dropped.

\emph{The Euler product.} By absolute convergence, $\mathcal A(m)$ factors over primes, the argument of \cref{lem:euler} applying verbatim to the double sum. Only the exponents $0$ and $1$ occur in $e_1,e_2$. At a prime $p$ the four choices contribute $1$, $-p^{-2}$, $-p^{-2}$, and $p^{-2}\one_{p^2\mid m}$, so by \eqref{eq:sing}
\[
\mathcal A(m)=\prod_p\Bigl(1-\frac2{p^2}+\frac{\one_{p^2\mid m}}{p^2}\Bigr)=\Sing(m).
\]

\emph{Conclusion.} Since $0\le L\le x$, $H\ge\frac12x^{2/5}$ and $\log H\le\frac25\log x\le2x^{1/5}$ (\cref{lem:sums}(d)), \eqref{eq:corr-tail} gives
\[
L\,|\mathcal A_H(m)-\Sing(m)|\le16x^{3/5}\bigl(2+\tfrac25\log x\bigr)\le32x^{3/5}+32x^{4/5}\le64x^{4/5}.
\]
Combining with \eqref{eq:corr-trunc} and \eqref{eq:corr-AH} gives $|C_x(m)-L\Sing(m)|\le(2+2D_{1/10}+1+64)x^{4/5}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The constant $D_{1/10}$ is astronomically large, but only its existence matters. For the application in \cref{ch:assembly} only the exponent $4/5$ is relevant: summing the error over the roughly $x\log x$ weighted pairs $(h,k)$ costs $x^{9/5+\eps}$, and this caps the saving in \cref{thm:main} at $\eta_d\le1/5$.
\end{remark}

\chapter{The Dirichlet series of the averaged singular series}\label{ch:dirichlet}

The averaged singular series $W_f(h)$ is a product of local factors that depend only on whether $p\nmid h$, $p\parallel h$ or $p^2\mid h$. Its Dirichlet series therefore factors as $\zeta(s)$ times an Euler product, and a second factorisation exhibits a pole of $\zeta(2s+2)$ at $s=-\frac12$. This pole, which is invisible in the region of absolute convergence, produces the main term of \cref{thm:main}. (Archived source: proposition P5, a crux of the plan.)

\section{Facts about the zeta function}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:zeta}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item For every integer $N\ge1$ and $\re w>0$, $w\ne1$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zeta-N}
\zeta(w)=\sum_{n\le N}n^{-w}+\frac{N^{1-w}}{w-1}-w\int_N^\infty\{u\}u^{-w-1}\dd u.
\end{equation}
In $\re w>0$ the only pole of $\zeta$ is a simple pole at $w=1$ with residue $1$, and $\lim_{w\to1}\bigl(\zeta(w)-\frac1{w-1}\bigr)=\gamma_E$, Euler's constant.
\item Let $\mathcal B_2(u)=\{u\}^2-\{u\}+\frac16$. The formula
\begin{equation}\label{eq:zeta-B2}
\zeta(w)=\frac1{w-1}+\frac12+\frac w{12}-\frac{w(w+1)}2\int_1^\infty\mathcal B_2(u)u^{-w-2}\dd u
\end{equation}
continues $\zeta$ meromorphically to $\re w>-1$, with the single pole at $w=1$. In particular $\zeta(0)=-\frac12$ and $-\frac14\le\zeta(-\frac12)\le-\frac16$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(a) For $\re w>1$ write $\sum_{n>N}n^{-w}=\int_N^\infty u^{-w}\dd\lfloor u\rfloor$, replace $\lfloor u\rfloor=u-\{u\}$, and integrate the second part by parts; the boundary term at $N$ vanishes because $\{N\}=0$. This gives \eqref{eq:zeta-N}. The integral converges absolutely and locally uniformly for $\re w>0$ because $0\le\{u\}<1$, which gives the continuation and the description of the pole. Subtracting $1/(w-1)$ and letting $w\to1$ gives $\sum_{n\le N}n^{-1}-\log N-\int_N^\infty\{u\}u^{-2}\dd u$. The integral lies in $[0,1/N]$, and letting $N\to\infty$ gives $\gamma_E$ by its definition as $\lim(\sum_{n\le N}n^{-1}-\log N)$.

(b) With $N=1$, \eqref{eq:zeta-N} becomes $\zeta(w)=\frac1{w-1}+\frac12-w\int_1^\infty(\{u\}-\frac12)u^{-w-1}\dd u$. The function $\mathcal B_2$ is continuous, satisfies $|\mathcal B_2|\le\frac16$, and has derivative $2(\{u\}-\frac12)$ between integers. Integration by parts over $[1,T]$ for integers $T\ge2$ gives
\[
\int_1^T\Bigl(\{u\}-\frac12\Bigr)u^{-w-1}\dd u=\frac{T^{-w-1}-1}{12}+\frac{w+1}2\int_1^T\mathcal B_2(u)u^{-w-2}\dd u,
\]
and letting $T\to\infty$ for $\re w>1$ yields \eqref{eq:zeta-B2}. The last integral and its $w$-derivatives are dominated on $\re w\ge\sigma_0>-1$ by $(\log u)^mu^{-\sigma_0-2}/6$, so the integral is holomorphic for $\re w>-1$. At $w=0$ the formula gives $-1+\frac12=-\frac12$. At $w=-\frac12$ it gives $\zeta(-\frac12)=-\frac5{24}+\frac18\int_1^\infty\mathcal B_2(u)u^{-3/2}\dd u$, and the integral has modulus at most $\frac16\cdot2=\frac13$.
\end{proof}

\section{The local factors}

For each prime $p$ put
\begin{equation}\label{eq:abd}
\alpha_p=1-\frac2{p^2}+\frac{\rho(p^2)}{p^4},\qquad
\beta_p=\frac{\rho(p)}{p^3}-\frac{\rho(p^2)}{p^4},\qquad
\delta_p=\frac1{p^2}-\frac{\rho(p)}{p^3}.
\end{equation}
By \eqref{eq:W-intro}, $w_p(h)$ equals $\alpha_p$, $\alpha_p+\beta_p$ or $\alpha_p+\beta_p+\delta_p$ according as $p\nmid h$, $p\parallel h$ or $p^2\mid h$. By \cref{lem:rho} (with $C_\rho=\max\{d,|A_d|\}$),
\begin{equation}\label{eq:abd-bounds}
1-\frac2{p^2}\le\alpha_p\le1,\quad0\le\beta_p\le\frac{\rho(p)}{p^3}\le\frac{C_\rho}{p^3},\quad0\le\delta_p\le\frac1{p^2},\quad\alpha_p+\beta_p+\delta_p=1-\frac1{p^2}.
\end{equation}
Here $\beta_p\ge0$ because $\rho(p^2)\le p\rho(p)$, $\delta_p\ge0$ because $\rho(p)\le p$, and $\alpha_p\le1$ because $\rho(p^2)\le p^2$. Define
\begin{equation}\label{eq:LM}
L_p(s)=\alpha_p+\beta_pp^{-s}+\delta_pp^{-2s},\qquad
M_p(s)=\bigl(1-p^{-2s-2}\bigr)L_p(s),
\end{equation}
and $P_f(s)=\prod_pL_p(s)$, $H_f(s)=\prod_pM_p(s)$. In the notation of the archived proposition, $L_p(s)=(1-2p^{-2})B_p(s)$ with $B_p(s)=a_p+(b_p-a_p)p^{-s}+(c_p-b_p)p^{-2s}$, $a_p=1+\rho(p^2)/(p^2(p^2-2))$, $b_p=1+\rho(p)/(p(p^2-2))$, $c_p=1+1/(p^2-2)$. Expanding the product, with $z=p^{-s}$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:M-expand}
M_p(s)=\alpha_p+\beta_pz+\bigl(\delta_p-\alpha_pp^{-2}\bigr)z^2-\beta_pp^{-2}z^3-\delta_pp^{-2}z^4,\qquad
\delta_p-\alpha_pp^{-2}=-\frac{\rho(p)}{p^3}+\frac2{p^4}-\frac{\rho(p^2)}{p^6}.
\end{equation}
The cancellation of the terms $\pm p^{-2}$ in the coefficient of $z^2$ is what allows $H_f$ to converge beyond $\re s=-\frac12$.

\section{The factorisation}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:Df}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item The series $D_f(s)=\sum_hW_f(h)h^{-s}$ converges absolutely for $\re s>1$.
\item The products $P_f$ and $H_f$ converge absolutely and locally uniformly, and define holomorphic functions, on $\re s>-\frac12$ and $\re s>-\frac34$ respectively. For every fixed $\sigma>-\frac34$ there is $C(f,\sigma)$ with $|H_f(\sigma+it)|\le C(f,\sigma)$ for all real $t$.
\item For $\re s>1$, $D_f(s)=\zeta(s)P_f(s)=\zeta(s)\zeta(2s+2)H_f(s)$. The function $\zeta(s)\zeta(2s+2)H_f(s)$ continues $D_f$ meromorphically to $\re s>-\frac34$, and its only possible poles there are simple poles at $s=1$ and $s=-\frac12$.
\item $R_f=\Res_{s=1}D_f(s)=P_f(1)$. Moreover $R_f=\widetilde R_f$, where $\widetilde R_f$ is the series of \cref{lem:W-basic}, and \eqref{eq:R-intro} holds. Moreover $B_f=(\gamma_E-\frac32)R_f+P_f'(1)$, and $R_f>0$.
\item $D_f(0)=-\frac1{2\zeta(2)}=-\frac c2$.
\item We have $\Res_{s=-1/2}D_f(s)=\frac12\zeta(-\frac12)H_f(-\frac12)$, and hence
\[
K_f=-\tfrac83\,\zeta(-\tfrac12)\,H_f(-\tfrac12).
\]
Moreover $H_f(-\frac12)>0$ and $K_f>0$, and $K_f$ is given by the absolutely convergent product \eqref{eq:K-intro}.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(a) By \cref{lem:W-basic}, $0<W_f(h)\le1$, so $\sum_h|W_f(h)h^{-s}|\le\sum_hh^{-\re s}$.

(b) For $\re s\ge\sigma$, \eqref{eq:abd-bounds} gives $|L_p(s)-1|\le2p^{-2}+C_\rho p^{-3-\sigma}+p^{-2-2\sigma}$. If $\sigma>-\frac12$ all exponents exceed one, so by \cref{lem:sums}(c) the sum over $p$ converges uniformly on $\re s\ge\sigma$, and \cref{lem:products} applies. For $M_p$, \eqref{eq:M-expand} and \eqref{eq:abd-bounds} give, for $\re s\ge\sigma$,
\[
|M_p(s)-1|\le\frac2{p^2}+\frac{C_\rho}{p^{3+\sigma}}+\Bigl(\frac{C_\rho}{p^3}+\frac2{p^4}+\frac{C_\rho}{p^5}\Bigr)p^{-2\sigma}+\frac{C_\rho}{p^{5+3\sigma}}+\frac1{p^{4+4\sigma}}.
\]
For $\sigma>-\frac34$ the exponents $2$, $3+\sigma$, $3+2\sigma$, $4+2\sigma$, $5+2\sigma$, $5+3\sigma$ and $4+4\sigma$ all exceed one. Hence $\sum_p\sup_{\re s\ge\sigma}|M_p(s)-1|=:E_H(\sigma)<\infty$, and \cref{lem:products} gives absolute and locally uniform convergence, holomorphy, and $|H_f(\sigma+it)|\le\exp(E_H(\sigma))$ for all $t$.

(c) By \cref{lem:W-basic}, $W_f(1)=\prod_p\alpha_p>0$, and $u(h)=W_f(h)/W_f(1)$ is multiplicative with $u(p)=(\alpha_p+\beta_p)/\alpha_p$ and $u(p^e)=(\alpha_p+\beta_p+\delta_p)/\alpha_p$ for $e\ge2$. For $\re s>1$ and $z=p^{-s}$ its local series is
\[
1+\frac{\alpha_p+\beta_p}{\alpha_p}z+\frac{\alpha_p+\beta_p+\delta_p}{\alpha_p}\cdot\frac{z^2}{1-z}=\frac{\alpha_p+\beta_pz+\delta_pz^2}{\alpha_p(1-z)}=\frac{L_p(s)}{\alpha_p(1-p^{-s})}.
\]
Since $\sum_h|u(h)h^{-s}|<\infty$, \cref{lem:euler} gives $\sum_hu(h)h^{-s}=\prod_pL_p(s)\alpha_p^{-1}(1-p^{-s})^{-1}$. Multiplying by the convergent product $W_f(1)=\prod_p\alpha_p$ and using the Euler product of $\zeta$ gives $D_f(s)=\zeta(s)P_f(s)$; the limits of partial products multiply. For $\re s>-\frac12$ we have $\re(2s+2)>1$, so $\prod_p(1-p^{-2s-2})^{-1}=\zeta(2s+2)$ converges absolutely, and multiplying the products gives $P_f(s)=\zeta(2s+2)H_f(s)$. By \cref{lem:zeta}(b), $\zeta(s)$ and $\zeta(2s+2)$ are meromorphic for $\re s>-\frac34$, since then $\re s>-1$ and $\re(2s+2)>\frac12$. Their only poles there are at $s=1$ and at $2s+2=1$, both simple. Since $H_f$ is holomorphic, $\zeta(s)\zeta(2s+2)H_f(s)$ is the required continuation.

(d) Since $P_f$ is holomorphic near $1$ and $\zeta(s)=\frac1{s-1}+\gamma_E+O(s-1)$ by \cref{lem:zeta}(a), we have $D_f(s)=\frac{P_f(1)}{s-1}+\gamma_EP_f(1)+P_f'(1)+O(s-1)$. This gives $R_f=P_f(1)$ and the formula for $B_f$ from \eqref{eq:RBK}. Substituting \eqref{eq:abd},
\[
L_p(1)=\alpha_p+\frac{\beta_p}p+\frac{\delta_p}{p^2}=1-\frac2{p^2}+\frac1{p^4}+\frac{(1-1/p)(\rho(p)+\rho(p^2))}{p^4}
=\Bigl(1-\frac1{p^2}\Bigr)^2\bigl(1+T_p\bigr),
\]
with $T_p=(1-1/p)(\rho(p)+\rho(p^2))/(p^2-1)^2\ge0$. By \cref{lem:rho}(d) and $p^2-1\ge\frac34p^2$, $T_p\le\frac{16C_\rho}9(p^{-3}+p^{-4})$, which is summable. Using \eqref{eq:c-euler}, $R_f=\zeta(2)^{-2}\prod_p(1+T_p)>0$, which is \eqref{eq:R-intro}. For the identification with $\widetilde R_f$, note that $\lambda(r)\rho(r)/r=c^2v(r)$, where $v$ is the nonnegative multiplicative function supported on cube-free integers with
\[
v(p)=\frac{(1-1/p)\rho(p)}{(p^2-1)^2},\qquad v(p^2)=\frac{\varphi(p^2)\rho(p^2)}{p^2(p^2-1)^2}=\frac{(1-1/p)\rho(p^2)}{(p^2-1)^2},
\]
using \cref{lem:rho}(a) for multiplicativity of $\rho$. Thus $1+v(p)+v(p^2)=1+T_p$. Since $\sum_rv(r)<\infty$ by \cref{lem:W-basic}, \cref{lem:euler} gives $\widetilde R_f=c^2\prod_p(1+T_p)=R_f$.

(e) $L_p(0)=\alpha_p+\beta_p+\delta_p=1-p^{-2}$, so $P_f(0)=c$ by \eqref{eq:c-euler}. The identity $D_f=\zeta P_f$ persists on $\re s>-\frac12$ by (c), and both factors are regular at $0$; by \cref{lem:zeta}(b), $D_f(0)=\zeta(0)c=-c/2$.

(f) Near $s=-\frac12$, $\zeta(2s+2)=\frac{1/2}{s+1/2}+O(1)$, while $\zeta(s)$ and $H_f(s)$ are holomorphic. This gives the residue, and the formula for $K_f$ follows from \eqref{eq:RBK}. At $s=-\frac12$,
\[
M_p(-\tfrac12)=\Bigl(1-\frac1p\Bigr)\bigl(\alpha_p+\beta_p\sqrt p+\delta_pp\bigr)=\Bigl(1-\frac1p\Bigr)\Bigl(1+\frac1p-\frac{2+\rho(p)}{p^2}+\frac{\rho(p)}{p^{5/2}}-\frac{\rho(p^2)}{p^{7/2}}+\frac{\rho(p^2)}{p^4}\Bigr),
\]
by direct substitution of \eqref{eq:abd}. This is positive because $\alpha_p>0$ and $\beta_p,\delta_p\ge0$. The deviations $|M_p(-\frac12)-1|$ are summable by (b), so \cref{lem:products}(c) gives $H_f(-\frac12)>0$, and \cref{lem:zeta}(b) gives $\zeta(-\frac12)<0$. Hence $K_f>0$, and \eqref{eq:K-intro} is the product $H_f(-\frac12)=\prod_pM_p(-\frac12)$, with the factors at each prime kept together.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Both poles in (c) are genuine, since $R_f>0$ and $K_f>0$. The value $D_f(0)=-c/2$ is essential: in \cref{ch:mellin} it produces the term $+cH$ in the expansion of $\Theta_f(H)$, which cancels the diagonal term $cx(Y-Z)$ of \cref{prop:reduction}. The identity $R_f=\widetilde R_f$ makes the logarithmic terms cancel as well.
\end{remark}
\chapter{The smoothed sum \texorpdfstring{$\Theta_f$}{Theta\_f} and the profile \texorpdfstring{$F_f$}{F\_f}}\label{ch:mellin}

The integral $I_f(x;Z,Y)$ of \cref{prop:averaging} turns out to be an exact combination of values of the smoothed partial sum
\[
\Theta_f(H)=\sum_{1\le h\le H}W_f(h)\frac{(H-h)^2}{h}.
\]
We evaluate $\Theta_f(H)$ by a Mellin integral of $D_f$ and a contour shift past the poles at $s=1$, $s=0$ (from the kernel) and $s=-\frac12$. The residues give the four terms $R_fH^2\log H$, $B_fH^2$, $cH$ and $-K_fH^{1/2}$. (Archived source: proposition P6.)

\section{Growth in a vertical strip}

We need one result from the literature. It is a consequence of the functional equation $\zeta(s)=\zeta(1-s)2^s\pi^{s-1}\Gamma(1-s)\sin(\pi s/2)$ \cite[Corollary~10.4]{montgomeryvaughan2007} and Stirling's formula.

\begin{quoted}[{\cite[Corollary~10.5]{montgomeryvaughan2007}}]\label{quoted:mv}
Suppose that $A>0$ is fixed. Then $|\zeta(s)|\asymp\tau^{1/2-\sigma}|\zeta(1-s)|$ uniformly for $|\sigma|\le A$ and $|t|\ge1$. Here $s=\sigma+it$ and $\tau=|t|+4$.
\end{quoted}

We use it with $A=2$, only for $-\frac58\le\sigma\le\frac14$ and $|t|\ge2$. Let $c_{\mathrm{MV}}$ be a constant for the upper inequality there, so that $|\zeta(s)|\le c_{\mathrm{MV}}(|t|+4)^{1/2-\sigma}|\zeta(1-s)|$. No conditional estimate for $\zeta$ is used. (The holomorphy of $\zeta$ in the strip away from $s=1$ is already contained in \cref{lem:zeta}(b).)

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:zeta-growth}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*)]
\item Let $\delta\in\{\frac14,\frac34\}$ and $C_\delta=(\frac32)^{1-\delta}\bigl(\frac1{1-\delta}+1\bigr)+\frac4\delta$. Then $|\zeta(\alpha+iv)|\le C_\delta|v|^{1-\delta}$ for $\delta\le\alpha\le6$ and $|v|\ge2$.
\item For $s=\sigma+it$ with $-\frac58\le\sigma\le2$ and $T=|t|\ge2$,
\[
|\zeta(s)|\le C_\zeta T^{11/8},\qquad|\zeta(2s+2)|\le C_{3/4}(2T)^{1/4},\qquad C_\zeta=\max\{C_{1/4},\,3^{9/8}c_{\mathrm{MV}}C_{3/4}\}.
\]
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
(a) Let $V=|v|$ and use \eqref{eq:zeta-N} with $N=\lceil V\rceil$, so $V\le N\le\frac32V$. The finite sum has modulus at most $\sum_{n\le N}n^{-\delta}\le N^{1-\delta}/(1-\delta)\le(\frac32)^{1-\delta}V^{1-\delta}/(1-\delta)$. Next, $|N^{1-s}/(s-1)|\le N^{1-\delta}/V\le(\frac32)^{1-\delta}V^{1-\delta}$. Finally $|s\int_N^\infty\{u\}u^{-s-1}\dd u|\le|s|N^{-\alpha}/\alpha\le(V+6)V^{-\delta}/\delta\le4V^{1-\delta}/\delta$.

(b) For $\frac14\le\sigma\le2$ apply (a) with $\delta=\frac14$: $|\zeta(s)|\le C_{1/4}T^{3/4}\le C_{1/4}T^{11/8}$. For $-\frac58\le\sigma\le\frac14$, $\re(1-s)\in[\frac34,\frac{13}8]$, so (a) with $\delta=\frac34$ and \cref{quoted:mv} give $|\zeta(s)|\le c_{\mathrm{MV}}(3T)^{1/2-\sigma}C_{3/4}T^{1/4}\le3^{9/8}c_{\mathrm{MV}}C_{3/4}T^{11/8}$, using $T+4\le3T$ and $\frac14\le\frac12-\sigma\le\frac98$. Finally, $\re(2s+2)\in[\frac34,6]$ and $|\im(2s+2)|=2T\ge4$, so (a) with $\delta=\frac34$ applies.
\end{proof}

The bound of \cref{prop:Df}(b) for $H_f$ holds on each vertical line, but the contour shift needs a bound that is uniform on a strip. The archived proof supplies it directly from the Euler product.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:H-strip}
Let $\mathcal B$ be the finite set of primes dividing every coefficient of $f$. For $-\frac58\le\re s\le2$,
\[
|H_f(s)|\le M_H:=e^{8(1+d)}\prod_{p\in\mathcal B}\bigl(1+p^{-3/4}\bigr)\bigl(1+p^{5/8}+p^{5/4}\bigr).
\]
Consequently, with $C_D=2^{1/4}M_HC_\zeta C_{3/4}$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:D-growth}
|D_f(\sigma+it)|\le C_D|t|^{13/8}\qquad\bigl(-\tfrac58\le\sigma\le2,\ |t|\ge2\bigr).
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $z=p^{-s}$, so $|z|\le p^{5/8}$. For $p\notin\mathcal B$, \cref{lem:rho}(c) gives $\rho(p)\le d$ and $\rho(p^2)\le dp$. Together with \eqref{eq:abd-bounds} this yields $|\alpha_p-1|\le2p^{-2}$, $\beta_p\le dp^{-3}$, $\delta_p\le p^{-2}$ and $|\delta_p-\alpha_pp^{-2}|\le dp^{-3}+2p^{-4}+dp^{-5}\le(2d+1)p^{-3}$. By \eqref{eq:M-expand},
\[
|M_p(s)-1|\le2p^{-2}+dp^{-19/8}+(2d+1)p^{-7/4}+dp^{-25/8}+p^{-3/2}\le(4+4d)p^{-3/2},
\]
and $\sum_p(4+4d)p^{-3/2}\le(4+4d)\int_1^\infty u^{-3/2}\dd u=8(1+d)$. Hence $|\prod_{p\notin\mathcal B}M_p(s)|\le\prod(1+|M_p(s)-1|)\le e^{8(1+d)}$. For the finitely many $p\in\mathcal B$ use $0\le\alpha_p,\beta_p,\delta_p\le1$ to get $|M_p(s)|\le(1+p^{-2}|z|^2)(1+|z|+|z|^2)\le(1+p^{-3/4})(1+p^{5/8}+p^{5/4})$. The bound \eqref{eq:D-growth} then follows from $D_f(s)=\zeta(s)\zeta(2s+2)H_f(s)$ and \cref{lem:zeta-growth}(b).
\end{proof}

\section{The Mellin representation}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:kernel}
For real $v>0$,
\[
\frac1{2\pi i}\int_{1-i\infty}^{1+i\infty}\frac{2v^z}{z(z+1)(z+2)}\dd z=\begin{cases}(1-v^{-1})^2,&v\ge1,\\0,&0<v<1.\end{cases}
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The integral converges absolutely. For $v\ge1$, close the contour by a rectangle to the left, with horizontal sides at heights $\pm T$ and a left side on $\re z=-N-\frac12$ with an integer $N\ge3$. On the horizontal sides the integrand is $O_v(T^{-3})$, so their contribution tends to zero as $T\to\infty$. On the left side each of $|z|,|z+1|,|z+2|$ is at least $\sqrt{(N-3/2)^2+(\im z)^2}$, so the normalised integral is at most $\frac{v^{-N-1/2}}\pi\int_{\R}((N-\frac32)^2+t^2)^{-3/2}\dd t=\frac{2v^{-N-1/2}}{\pi(N-3/2)^2}$, which tends to zero also when $v=1$. The residues at $z=0,-1,-2$ are $1$, $-2v^{-1}$ and $v^{-2}$, with sum $(1-v^{-1})^2$. For $v<1$, close to the right instead; the right side $\re z=N$ contributes at most $2v^N/(\pi N^2)\to0$, and there are no poles.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:Theta}
There is $C(f)>0$ such that, uniformly for real $H\ge1$,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:Theta}
\Bigl|\Theta_f(H)-\bigl(R_fH^2\log H+B_fH^2+cH-K_fH^{1/2}\bigr)\Bigr|\le C(f)H^{3/8},
\end{equation}
and consequently
\begin{equation}\label{eq:F-asym}
\bigl|F_f(H)-K_fH^{-3/2}\bigr|\le C(f)H^{-13/8},\qquad|F_f(H)|\le(K_f+C(f))H^{-3/2}.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Mellin representation.} Apply \cref{lem:kernel} with $v=H/h$, multiply by $H^2W_f(h)/h$ and sum over $h\ge1$; note $H^2(1-h/H)^2=(H-h)^2$, and a term with $h=H$ vanishes on both sides. With $s=z+1$ the integrand becomes $2W_f(h)H^{s+1}h^{-s}/((s-1)s(s+1))$ on $\re s=2$. The sum over $h$ of its absolute values is at most $2H^3\zeta(2)/|(s-1)s(s+1)|$, which is integrable on the line, because $|(s-1)s(s+1)|\ge|\im s|^3$ there and the denominator does not vanish. Hence summation and integration may be interchanged, and
\[
\Theta_f(H)=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_{2-i\infty}^{2+i\infty}G_H(s)\dd s,\qquad G_H(s)=\frac{2D_f(s)H^{s+1}}{(s-1)s(s+1)}.
\]

\emph{Contour shift.} In the strip $-\frac58\le\re s\le2$ the poles of $G_H$ are at $s=1$ and $s=-\frac12$ (from $D_f$, \cref{prop:Df}(c)) and at $s=0$ (from the kernel); the pole of the kernel at $-1$ lies outside. On the horizontal segments at height $\pm T$, $T\ge2$, each factor of the denominator has modulus at least $T$, and $|H^{s+1}|\le H^3$. So \eqref{eq:D-growth} bounds the integrand by $2C_DH^3T^{-11/8}$. The segments have length $\frac{21}8$, so their total contribution is at most $\frac{21}2C_DH^3T^{-11/8}\to0$ as $T\to\infty$, for each fixed $H$. On the line $\re s=-\frac58$ we have $|(s-1)s(s+1)|\ge\frac{13}8\cdot\frac58\cdot\frac38=\frac{195}{512}$. With $M_0=\max_{|t|\le2}|D_f(-\frac58+it)|$, finite because the line contains no pole, the part $|t|\le2$ contributes at most $4\cdot\frac{512}{195}\cdot2M_0H^{3/8}$ to the integral of $|G_H|$. For $|t|\ge2$, \eqref{eq:D-growth} gives $|G_H|\le2C_DH^{3/8}|t|^{-11/8}$, and these tails contribute at most $4C_DH^{3/8}\int_2^\infty t^{-11/8}\dd t=\frac{32}3C_D2^{-3/8}H^{3/8}$. Hence the residue theorem gives
\[
\Theta_f(H)=\sum_{s_0\in\{1,0,-1/2\}}\Res_{s=s_0}G_H(s)+E_f(H),\qquad|E_f(H)|\le C_*H^{3/8},
\]
where $C_*=\frac1{2\pi}\bigl(\frac{4096}{195}M_0+\frac{32}3C_D2^{-3/8}\bigr)$ depends only on $f$.

\emph{Residues.} Write $D_f(s)=\frac{R_f}{s-1}+L_f+O(s-1)$ near $s=1$, so that $B_f=L_f-\frac32R_f$ by \eqref{eq:RBK}, and put $q(s)=2/(s(s+1))$. Then $q(1)=1$ and $q'(1)=-\frac32$, and $G_H(s)=q(s)H^{s+1}D_f(s)/(s-1)$ has the residue
\begin{align*}
\Res_{s=1}G_H&=R_f\frac{\dd}{\dd s}\bigl(q(s)H^{s+1}\bigr)\Big|_{s=1}+L_fq(1)H^2\\
&=H^2\bigl(R_f\log H-\tfrac32R_f+L_f\bigr)=R_fH^2\log H+B_fH^2.
\end{align*}
At $s=0$, \cref{prop:Df}(e) gives
\[
\Res_{s=0}G_H=\frac{2D_f(0)H}{(-1)\cdot1}=-2D_f(0)H=cH.
\]
At $s=-\frac12$ the denominator equals $(-\frac32)(-\frac12)(\frac12)=\frac38$, so by \eqref{eq:RBK}
\[
\Res_{s=-1/2}G_H=\frac{16}3H^{1/2}\Res_{s=-1/2}D_f=-K_fH^{1/2}.
\]

This proves \eqref{eq:Theta} with $C(f)=1+C_*$, for every real $H\ge1$, integral or not. Substituting into \eqref{eq:ThetaF} gives the exact identity $F_f(H)=K_fH^{-3/2}-E_f(H)/H^2$, which implies \eqref{eq:F-asym} since $H^{-13/8}\le H^{-3/2}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The integral identity}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:integral-identity}
For real $x\ge2$ and $2\le Z\le Y\le x$,
\[
2\int_Z^Y\sum_{1\le h\le x/t}(x-ht)W_f(h)\dd t=Z^2\Theta_f(x/Z)-Y^2\Theta_f(x/Y).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Write $u_+=\max\{u,0\}$. For $t\in[Z,Y]$ only $h\le x/Z$ contribute, so the left side equals $\sum_{h\le x/Z}W_f(h)\cdot2\int_Z^Y(x-ht)_+\dd t$. The function $t\mapsto(x-ht)_+^2$ is continuously differentiable with derivative $-2h(x-ht)_+$, hence $2\int_Z^Y(x-ht)_+\dd t=\bigl((x-hZ)_+^2-(x-hY)_+^2\bigr)/h$. On the other hand, by the definition of $\Theta_f$, $Z^2\Theta_f(x/Z)=\sum_{h\le x/Z}W_f(h)(x-hZ)_+^2/h$ and $Y^2\Theta_f(x/Y)=\sum_{h\le x/Z}W_f(h)(x-hY)_+^2/h$; in the second sum the terms with $h>x/Y$ vanish. Subtracting gives the identity, with no error term. Note that $x/Z,x/Y\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\chapter{Proof of the main theorem}\label{ch:assembly}

We now combine \cref{prop:reduction,prop:initial,prop:corr,prop:averaging,prop:Df,prop:Theta} and \cref{lem:integral-identity}. Throughout, $d=\deg f$,
\[
a=4d-1,\qquad\theta=\frac{2d}a,\qquad\eta=\eta_d=\min\Bigl\{\frac15,\frac1a\Bigr\},
\]
and $\eps$ with $0<\eps<\eta/2$ is fixed. We put $\eps_1=\eps/2$ and apply \cref{prop:reduction,prop:initial} with $\eps_1$ in place of $\eps$. Constants written $C_f$ depend only on $f$, constants written $C_{f,\eps}$ depend only on $f$ and $\eps$, and both may change from line to line. (Archived source: proposition P7.)

\section{The variance on an interval of moduli}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:interval}
Let $x\ge2$, $1\le z\le y$, $2\le Z=f(z)\le Y=f(y)\le x$ and $2\le R\le x$, and let $\mathcal A=\mathcal A(x,Z,R)$ be as in \cref{prop:averaging}. Then
\[
\Bigl|V_f(x;z,y)-x^2\bigl(F_f(x/Y)-F_f(x/Z)\bigr)\Bigr|\le C_{f,\eps}\bigl(x^{3/2+\eps_1}+xY^{1-1/d}+x^{9/5}\log(2x)+x^2\mathcal A\bigr).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
\emph{Correlations.} For $z<k\le y$ there are at most $x/f(k)$ integers $h$ with $1\le h\le x/f(k)$, and each $m=hf(k)$ is an integer in $[1,x]$. By \cref{prop:corr}, replacing $C_x(m)$ by $(x-m)\Sing(m)$ costs at most $K_{\mathrm c}x^{4/5}$ for each pair $(h,k)$. After multiplying by $f'(k)$ and summing over $h$, the cost is at most $K_{\mathrm c}x^{9/5}f'(k)/f(k)$, and by \eqref{eq:sum-w}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:S-M}
|S_f(x;z,y)-M_f(x;z,y)|\le2UK_{\mathrm c}\,x^{9/5}\log(2x).
\end{equation}
\emph{Averaging.} \Cref{prop:averaging} applies under exactly the present hypotheses and gives
\[
|M_f(x;z,y)-I_f(x;Z,Y)|\le C_fx^2\mathcal A,\qquad|T_f(z,y)-R_f\log(Y/Z)|\le C_f\mathcal A,
\]
where we used $\widetilde R_f=R_f$ (\cref{prop:Df}(d)).

\emph{Combination.} By \cref{prop:reduction}, the factor $2$ in front of $S_f$ and the factor $x^2$ in front of $T_f$ give
\begin{multline*}
\Bigl|V_f(x;z,y)-\bigl[cx(Y-Z)+2I_f(x;Z,Y)-x^2R_f\log(Y/Z)\bigr]\Bigr|\\
\le C_{f,\eps}\bigl(x^{3/2+\eps_1}+xY^{1-1/d}\bigr)+4UK_{\mathrm c}x^{9/5}\log(2x)+3C_fx^2\mathcal A.
\end{multline*}
\emph{Exact cancellation.} By \cref{lem:integral-identity}, $2I_f(x;Z,Y)=Z^2\Theta_f(x/Z)-Y^2\Theta_f(x/Y)$. By the definition \eqref{eq:ThetaF} of $F_f$, with $c=\zeta(2)^{-1}$, direct subtraction gives
\[
x^2\bigl(F_f(x/Y)-F_f(x/Z)\bigr)=-x^2R_f\log(Y/Z)+cx(Y-Z)+Z^2\Theta_f(x/Z)-Y^2\Theta_f(x/Y),
\]
because $x^2\cdot c/(x/Y)=cxY$ and the $B_f$-terms cancel. Hence the bracket equals $x^2(F_f(x/Y)-F_f(x/Z))$.
\end{proof}

\section{Choice of the cutoffs and the error budget}\label{sec:budget}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \eqref{eq:main1}]
Take
\[
Z=x^\theta=x^{2d/a},\qquad R=Z^{1/d}=x^{2/a}.
\]
Both exponents lie in $(0,1)$. Choose $x_0=x_0(f)\ge2$ so that $Z\ge\max\{2,f(1)\}$ and $R\ge2$ for $x\ge x_0$; then also $Z\le x$ and $R\le x$. By \cref{lem:poly}(a) there is a unique real $z\ge1$ with $f(z)=Z$. Then
\begin{equation}\label{eq:exponents}
\begin{gathered}
Z^{-1/d}R^{1/2}=R^{-1/2}=x^{-1/a},\qquad1+\theta\Bigl(2-\frac1d\Bigr)=\frac{8d-3}a=2-\frac1a,\\
\Bigl(2-\frac1a\Bigr)-\Bigl(\frac12+\frac{3d}a\Bigr)=\frac{6d-5}{2a}>0.
\end{gathered}
\end{equation}

\emph{Case $Y\ge Z$.} Then $z\le y$, and $V_f(x,y)=V_f(x,z)+V_f(x;z,y)$. Hence
\[
|V_f(x,y)-x^2F_f(x/Y)|\le V_f(x,z)+x^2|F_f(x/Z)|+\Bigl|V_f(x;z,y)-x^2\bigl(F_f(x/Y)-F_f(x/Z)\bigr)\Bigr|.
\]
We bound every term; the table summarises the result.
\begin{itemize}
\item By \cref{prop:initial} and \eqref{eq:exponents}, $V_f(x,z)\le C_{f,\eps}x^{1+\eps_1}Z^{2-1/d}=C_{f,\eps}x^{2-1/a+\eps_1}$.
\item By \eqref{eq:F-asym} with $H=x/Z\ge1$, $x^2|F_f(x/Z)|\le C_fx^{1/2}Z^{3/2}=C_fx^{1/2+3d/a}\le C_fx^{2-1/a}$, by \eqref{eq:exponents}.
\item By \cref{lem:interval} the remaining difference is at most $C_{f,\eps}$ times $x^{3/2+\eps_1}+xY^{1-1/d}+x^{9/5}\log(2x)+x^2\mathcal A$, and by \eqref{eq:exponents} and $R\le x$, $x^2\mathcal A\le x^{2-1/a}\bigl((\log2x)^2+(\log2x)^3\bigr)$.
\end{itemize}
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
origin & bound & comparison with $x^{2-\eta+\eps}$\\
\midrule
diagonal and mixed terms (\cref{prop:reduction}) & $x^{3/2+\eps_1}$ & $\eta\le\frac15<\frac12$\\
endpoint of $\sum f'(k)$ (\cref{prop:reduction}) & $xY^{1-1/d}\le x^{2-1/d}$ & $\eta\le\frac1a\le\frac1d$\\
correlations (\cref{prop:corr}) & $x^{9/5}\log(2x)$ & $\eta\le\frac15$, $\log2x\ll_\eps x^{\eps}$\\
averaging (\cref{prop:averaging}) & $x^{2-1/a}(\log2x)^3$ & $\eta\le\frac1a$, $(\log2x)^3\ll_\eps x^{\eps}$\\
initial moduli (\cref{prop:initial}) & $x^{2-1/a+\eps_1}$ & $\eta\le\frac1a$\\
lower boundary of the profile & $x^{1/2+3d/a}\le x^{2-1/a}$ & $\eta\le\frac1a$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
The logarithmic bounds come from \cref{lem:sums}(d). Every row is at most $C_{f,\eps}x^{2-\eta+\eps}$ for $x\ge2$, which proves \eqref{eq:main1} when $Y\ge Z$.

\emph{Case $Y<Z$.} Apply \cref{prop:initial} with $y$ in place of $z$; this is allowed since $y\ge1$ and $f(y)=Y\le x$. Because $2-1/d>0$,
\[
0\le V_f(x,y)\le C_{f,\eps}x^{1+\eps_1}Y^{2-1/d}\le C_{f,\eps}x^{1+\eps_1}Z^{2-1/d}=C_{f,\eps}x^{2-1/a+\eps_1}.
\]
Also $x/Y\ge1$, so \eqref{eq:F-asym} and \eqref{eq:exponents} give $x^2|F_f(x/Y)|\le C_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}\le C_fx^{1/2+3d/a}\le C_fx^{2-1/a}$. The triangle inequality and $\eta\le1/a$ give \eqref{eq:main1} in this case too.

All constants and $x_0$ depend only on $f$ and $\eps$, and real values of $y$ and $z$ need no separate treatment: the sums split exactly as $k\le z$ and $z<k\le y$, and the endpoint effects are contained in \cref{prop:reduction}.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}[Proof of \eqref{eq:main2}]
For every $Y\le x$, \eqref{eq:F-asym} with $H=x/Y\ge1$, multiplied by $x^2$, gives
\[
\bigl|x^2F_f(x/Y)-K_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}\bigr|\le C_fx^2(x/Y)^{-13/8}=C_fx^{3/8}Y^{13/8},
\]
since $x^2(x/Y)^{-3/2}=x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$. Combine this with \eqref{eq:main1}. The formulas \eqref{eq:K-intro} and \eqref{eq:R-intro} and the positivity of $K_f$ are \cref{prop:Df}(d),(f).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The hypothesis $\eps<\eta_d/2$ is not used in the argument above, which works for every $\eps>0$. We keep the hypothesis as stated.
\end{remark}

\section{\texorpdfstring{Proof of \cref{cor:range}}{Proof of Corollary 1.2}}\label{sec:corollary}

Let $0<\delta<\eta/3$, apply \eqref{eq:main2} with $\eps=\delta$ (admissible because $\delta<\eta/2$), and divide by $K_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2}$, which is positive by \cref{prop:Df}(f). For $x^{1-2\eta/3+\delta}\le Y\le x^{1-\delta}$ the two relative errors are
\[
\frac{x^{3/8}Y^{13/8}}{x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}}=\Bigl(\frac Yx\Bigr)^{1/8}\le x^{-\delta/8},\qquad
\frac{x^{2-\eta+\delta}}{x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}}\le x^{3/2-\eta+\delta-\frac32(1-\frac{2\eta}3+\delta)}=x^{-\delta/2}\le x^{-\delta/8}.
\]
Hence $|V_f(x,y)/(K_fx^{1/2}Y^{3/2})-1|\le C(f,\delta)x^{-\delta/8}$ for $x\ge x_0(f,\delta)$. The range is nonempty because $\delta<\eta/3$. \qed

\section{Where the exponents come from}

The cap $\eta_d\le\frac15$ comes from summing the uniform correlation error $x^{4/5}$ of \cref{prop:corr} over all pairs $(h,k)$; the weighted number of these pairs is $\sum_kf'(k)x/f(k)\asymp x\log x$. The exponent $\frac1{4d-1}$ comes from balancing two terms. The first is the bound $x^{1+\eps}Z^{2-1/d}$ for the initial moduli; it arises from multiplying the full-variance bound \eqref{eq:full-variance} by the largest weight $f'(k)\asymp Z^{1-1/d}$. The second is the averaging error $x^2Z^{-1/(2d)}$ of \cref{prop:averaging} at $R=Z^{1/d}$. Equating $1+\theta(2-\frac1d)$ with $2-\frac\theta{2d}$ gives $\theta=\frac{2d}{4d-1}$. Improving either the treatment of small moduli or the correlation estimate would widen the range of \cref{cor:range}. Neither improvement is attempted here.
\chapter{Numerical evidence}\label{ch:numerics}

This chapter compares \cref{thm:main} with exact computations of $V_f(x,y)$. The computations come from the numerical snapshot accepted for the archived run (\texttt{source/numerics/snapshot}). Every number in the tables and figures below is recomputed by \texttt{reproduce.py} from that snapshot's own source code and from the formulas of \cref{ch:dirichlet,ch:mellin}; see the \texttt{README.md} accompanying this document. None of the numbers is part of the proof. The fits and their uncertainty envelopes are heuristic.

\section{What is computed}

The snapshot treats $f(t)=t^2+1$, $t^3+2$ and $2t+1$, which satisfy \eqref{eq:f-hyp}, for
\[
x\in\{2.5,5\}\cdot10^5\cup\{1,2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256\}\cdot10^6
\]
and the target ratios $r\in\{0.01,0.02,0.05,0.1,0.2,0.4,0.7,1\}$; for each pair, $y$ is the largest integer with $f(y)\le rx$. At the two largest values of $x$ the extra ratio $r=0.005$ is also computed, as a diagnostic. The normalised value
\[
N_{\mathrm{data}}=\frac{V_f(x,y)}{x^{1/2}f(y)^{3/2}}
\]
is compared with the prediction
\[
N_{\mathrm{pred}}=F_f(H)H^{3/2}=\frac{x^2F_f(x/Y)}{x^{1/2}Y^{3/2}},\qquad H=\frac x{f(y)}.
\]
By \eqref{eq:F-asym}, $N_{\mathrm{pred}}\to K_f$ as $H\to\infty$.

\paragraph{Algorithm of the snapshot.} The computation follows the expansion of \cref{ch:variance} exactly, with no approximation in the counts.
\begin{itemize}
\item The squarefree indicator up to $x$ is sieved, and all correlations $C_x(m)$, $0\le m\le x$, are obtained at once from one number-theoretic-transform autocorrelation modulo the prime $3221225473$. Every correlation is at most $x$, which is below the prime, so no information is lost. Then $\sum_lQ(x;q,l)^2=Q(x;1,1)+2\sum_{m\ge1}C_x(mq)$ by \cref{lem:pairs}.
\item With $g(q,l)=c\,h(q,l)$, where $h$ is the rational number given by \eqref{eq:density-closed}, the coefficients $B_q=\sum_lQ(x;q,l)h(q,l)$ and $D_q=\sum_lh(q,l)^2$ are computed as exact rationals. $B_q$ is computed from the counts $S_a=Q(x;a,0)$ exactly as in the proof of \cref{lem:mixed}. $D_q$ is computed from the local factors of \cref{prop:second-moment}(a): $p(p^2+p-1)/((p-1)(p+1)^2)$ for $p\parallel q$ and $p^2/(p^2-1)$ for $p^2\mid q$, divided by $q$.
\item The weighted sums are combined as $V=\sum_kf'(k)\bigl(\sum_lQ^2-2cxB_{f(k)}+c^2x^2D_{f(k)}\bigr)$ in 70-digit decimal arithmetic, with $c=6/\pi^2$ evaluated to 85 digits. The integer parts are exact; $V$ itself is irrational, so it is represented by a 70-digit approximation.
\end{itemize}

\paragraph{Validation in the snapshot code.} The pipeline checks that the transform prime and its primitive root are valid. It checks the rational density formula against an independent enumeration of local residue conditions for all $1275$ pairs $(q,l)$ with $q\le50$, and against brute-force frequencies at $x=10^6$. It checks $\sum_lQ(x;q,l)=Q(x;1,1)$, and it compares, for every modulus at $x=257$, the exact $\sum_lQ^2$ and both rational coefficients with direct residue counts. For every production value of $x$ it also checks $C_x(0)$ and seven lags of the autocorrelation directly. All checks passed in the recomputation.

\paragraph{Reproduction.} \texttt{reproduce.py} copies the snapshot's \texttt{compute.py} and \texttt{counts.cpp} unchanged, after checking their SHA-256 hashes against the acceptance record, compiles the C++ worker locally, and runs the snapshot pipeline on the full grid. In the run used to prepare this document, all $270$ recomputed rows agreed exactly with the archived results: the integer counts were identical, and so were the 70-digit values of $V$. This comparison is recorded in \texttt{reproduction.json}.

\section{The constants}

\Cref{tab:constants} lists $R_f$, $B_f$, $H_f(-\frac12)$ and $K_f$, computed from the Euler products of \cref{prop:Df} over the primes $p\le\Pmax$. The root counts $\rho(p)$ and $\rho(p^2)$ are computed by direct enumeration for small $p$ (for $\rho(p^2)$, $p\le300$; for $\rho(p)$, $p\le1000$). For larger $p$ they follow from the classical rules: for $t^2+1$, $\rho(p)=2$ if $p\equiv1\bmod4$ and $0$ otherwise; for $t^3+2$, $\rho(p)=1$ if $p\equiv2\bmod3$, and if $p\equiv1\bmod3$, $\rho(p)=3$ or $0$ according as $(-2)^{(p-1)/3}\equiv1\bmod p$ or not; for $2t+1$ and $t+1$, $\rho(p)=1$. For these $p$ the roots are simple, so $\rho(p^2)=\rho(p)$ by Hensel's lemma. The rules are checked against the direct counts wherever both are available.

